_description:
WordPress 7.2 is planned to introduce `data-wp-html`, a new Interactivity API directive for rendering trusted HTML inside interactive regions, such as a formatted field inside a `data-wp-each` list. It complements `data-wp-text`, which always renders its value as text. The use case is described in [https://github.com/WordPress/gutenberg/issues/70531 Gutenberg #70531] and in the directives roadmap in [https://github.com/WordPress/gutenberg/discussions/52904 Gutenberg #52904].

The client implementation was merged in [https://github.com/WordPress/gutenberg/pull/83092 Gutenberg PR #83092]. This ticket adds the matching server-side processing to Core, so that the HTML is present in the server-rendered page and the client keeps it when it hydrates. The server part should ship together with the client or after it, never before.

=== Proposal ===

A new function marks HTML as trusted:

{{{
wp_interactivity_as_dangerous_html( string $html ): WP_Interactivity_Dangerous_HTML
}}}

It returns an opaque token. The token holds no HTML; the server records the HTML against the token's identity for the current request. When a `data-wp-html` reference resolves to such a token during directive processing, the element's content is replaced with that HTML, byte for byte.

{{{
<?php
wp_interactivity_state(
	'myPlugin',
	array(
		'descriptions' => array(
			'a' => wp_kses_post( $description_a ),
			'b' => wp_kses_post( $description_b ),
		),
		'description'  => function () {
			$state   = wp_interactivity_state();
			$context = wp_interactivity_get_context();
			$html    = $state['descriptions'][ $context['item']['id'] ] ?? null;
			return null === $html ? null : wp_interactivity_as_dangerous_html( $html );
		},
	)
);
?>
<ul data-wp-interactive="myPlugin">
	<template data-wp-each="state.items">
		<li data-wp-html="state.description">Loading…</li>
	</template>
</ul>
}}}

=== Behavior ===

 * Only tokens returned by `wp_interactivity_as_dangerous_html()` render. Strings, including sanitized ones, never render, and neither do cloned, unserialized or reconstructed objects.
 * The HTML is neither sanitized nor validated. Preparing it is the caller's responsibility, as with HTML echoed from a filter.
 * Directives inside the inserted HTML are not processed on the server.
 * The host element keeps its tag and attributes, including `data-wp-html`. Its other directives, and the rest of the region, are processed as usual. It works per item inside `data-wp-each`.
 * Neither the token nor its HTML is written to the Interactivity data printed for the client. The client creates its own token, for example in a derived-state getter. * When the directive does not render, the element keeps its existing content. A `_doing_it_wrong()` notice is raised when:
   * the value is not a token;
   * the element cannot hold content (void elements and `script`, `style`, `textarea`, `title`, `iframe`, `noembed`, `noframes`, `xmp`);
   * the element also carries `data-wp-text`, or is a `template` carrying `data-wp-each`.
 * `null` or unresolved values are silent.

=== Security ===

The token is the only trust boundary. The function's documentation states the risks: passing untrusted data creates an XSS vulnerability, malformed HTML can break the page, and tags such as `body` can affect the page's root elements. It also states that a value read from context must never be passed to the function, because any ancestor block can override context. Context should only select which value to show; the HTML itself should come from state.

=== Implementation notes ===

 * The processor writes an inert placeholder comment as the element's content and swaps the trusted HTML in with a single `strtr()` pass after directive processing finishes. Core therefore never tokenizes the trusted HTML, and the processing loop, `data-wp-each` and the balanced-tag helper are unchanged.
 * Tests are PHPUnit only. Hydration in the browser is covered by the Gutenberg PR.

When Ghostscript (`gs`) is not installed, running the "Media Handling" section of Site Health from the command line prints an error message coming from the shell:
{{{
sh: gs: not found
}}}
The "Ghostscript version" field is still correctly reported as "Not available", so nothing breaks. The output is only noisy.

== Cause ==
`WP_Debug_Data::get_wp_media()` runs `gs --version` through `exec()`:
{{{#!php
<?php
$gs = exec( 'gs --version' );
}}}

`exec()` only captures stdout. When `gs` does not exist, the shell (not PHP) writes `sh: gs: not found` to stderr, and a terminal shows it as is. The empty result is already handled just below (`empty( $gs )`), so the intent of the code is to report "Not available" quietly.
This probably goes unnoticed in a web request, where the stderr of the child process is not part of the page output.
This code has been there since this class was introduced in WordPress 5.2.

== Steps to reproduce ==
1. Use an environment where `gs` is not installed (`command -v gs` prints nothing).
2. Use a WP-CLI version that includes the `site-health` command. It is missing in WP-CLI 2.12.0 (stable), so I used the nightly build.
3. Run `wp site-health info wp-media`.
4. Observe `sh: gs: not found` printed above the table.

The same message appears in any command line context that calls `WP_Debug_Data::debug_data()`. I first noticed it while running the PHPUnit tests of a plugin.

'''Expected result:''' no output besides the table, and "Ghostscript version" shows "Not available".

'''Actual result:''' `sh: gs: not found` is printed on stderr.

Tested with: WordPress trunk (7.2.0 in `package.json`, commit c23817e), WP-CLI 3.0.0-alpha-dd11f5a (nightly), PHP 8.3.33, Docker (Linux), using the wordpress-develop local environment.

== Reproducing with PHPUnit ==
Save the following as `tests/phpunit/tests/admin/wpDebugData.php` and run `npm run test:php -- --filter Tests_Admin_wpDebugData`.
With the current code, the PHPUnit output contains `sh: 1: gs: not found` (the exact wording depends on the shell). With the proposed fix below, the output is clean and the test still passes.

{{{#!php
<?php
<?php
/**
 * @group admin
 * @group site-health
 */
class Tests_Admin_wpDebugData extends WP_UnitTestCase {

	public function test_debug_data_without_ghostscript() {
		require_once ABSPATH . 'wp-admin/includes/class-wp-debug-data.php';

		// Block the external request made by WP_Debug_Data.
		add_filter(
			'pre_http_request',
			function () {
				return new WP_Error( 'test_http_request', 'HTTP request disabled for this test.' );
			}
		);

		// Simulate "gs is not installed" with a PATH that does not contain it.
		$original_path = getenv( 'PATH' );
		putenv( 'PATH=/nonexistent' );

		try {
			$info = WP_Debug_Data::debug_data();
		} finally {
			putenv( 'PATH=' . $original_path );
		}

		// The test passes, but with the current code "sh: gs: not found" is printed.
		$this->assertSame(
			'not available',
			$info['wp-media']['fields']['ghostscript_version']['debug']
		);
	}
}
}}}


== Proposed fix ==

Check that `gs` exists before running it:


{{{#!php
<?php
$gs = exec( 'command -v gs >/dev/null && gs --version' );
}}}

`command -v` is a shell builtin specified by POSIX, whereas `which` is an external program that is not guaranteed to be installed. With this change, in my test environment, the message disappears and the field is still reported as "Not available".


== Notes ==

* The `wp-cli/site-health-command` package already works around this message in several of its acceptance tests (see the comments in `features/site-health.feature`: https://github.com/wp-cli/site-health-command/blob/main/features/site-health.feature), so other projects are affected too.
* As far as I can see, no existing core PHPUnit test covers this section of `WP_Debug_Data`. Since the message comes from a child process and not from PHP, I am not sure how to assert on it in an automated test. Suggestions are welcome.
* I will open a pull request with this change.
* Related: #61648 (where `get_wp_media()` was extracted; the `exec()` call itself is older and was only moved).

AI assistance: Claude (Anthropic) helped me understand the problem, look up the affected version and write this report.

Patch proposed by Chouby.

Follow-up to #64156 and #66224.

#64156 lets repeated `add_post_type_support()` calls merge their array arguments, and #66224 lets `remove_post_type_support()` remove individual sub-features. `post_type_supports()` still only answers whether the feature as a whole is supported.

To check whether a post type has `notes` enabled on the `editor` feature today, you have to reach into the raw arguments yourself:

{{{#!php
<?php
$supports = get_all_post_type_supports( 'custom' );
$has_notes = isset( $supports['editor'][0]['notes'] ) && $supports['editor'][0]['notes'];
}}}

This was suggested by @mamaduka on the #64156 PR.

== Proposal

Add an optional third parameter, `$sub_feature`:

{{{#!php
<?php
post_type_supports( 'custom', 'editor', 'notes' );
}}}

* For associative arguments, the sub-feature is supported when its key is set to a non-empty value, so `'notes' => false` counts as not supported.
* For list arguments (e.g. a `post-formats` style feature), the sub-feature is supported when the list contains it.
* If the feature isn't supported, or has no array arguments, the result is `false`.
* Without the third parameter, behavior is unchanged.

Follow-up to #64156, which lets repeated `add_post_type_support()` calls merge their array arguments instead of overwriting them.

With that in place, `remove_post_type_support()` is the odd one out. It can only remove a feature as a whole. The `editor` feature now has several sub-features (`default-mode`, `notes`, ...), so turning off just one of them means removing the whole feature and registering it again with everything you want to keep:

{{{#!php
<?php
add_post_type_support( 'custom', 'editor', array(
    'default-mode' => 'template-locked',
    'notes'        => true,
) );

// Today: to drop only 'notes'.
remove_post_type_support( 'custom', 'editor' );
add_post_type_support( 'custom', 'editor', array(
    'default-mode' => 'template-locked',
) );
}}}

This was raised by @wildworks on the #64156 PR.

== Proposal

Add an optional third parameter, `$sub_features` (string or array of strings):

{{{#!php
<?php
remove_post_type_support( 'custom', 'editor', 'notes' );
remove_post_type_support( 'custom', 'editor', array( 'notes', 'default-mode' ) );
}}}

* For associative arguments, the given keys are removed regardless of their value.
* For list arguments (e.g. a `post-formats` style feature), matching values are removed and the list is reindexed.
* The feature itself stays supported, even when all of its sub-features are removed.
* If the feature has no array arguments, or isn't registered, nothing happens.
* Without the third parameter, behavior is unchanged and the whole feature is removed.

Checking for individual sub-features in `post_type_supports()` (also brought up on #64156) is left for a separate ticket.

Recent Composer versions deprecate the `config.audit.*` keys in favor of `config.policy`. Editors that validate `composer.json` against the current Composer schema (such as VS Code) now show this warning on the `audit` block:

{{{
DEPRECATED: use 'config.policy' instead. All 'audit.*' keys still work as a fallback for now. See https://getcomposer.org/doc/06-config.md#policy
}}}

The current configuration:

{{{
"audit": { "ignore": [ "GHSA-hmqg-cxww-wqhq", "PKSA-rdkp-vv9z-mjkg" ] },
}}}

The equivalent using `config.policy`:

{{{
"policy": {
      "advisories": {
              "ignore-id": [ "GHSA-hmqg-cxww-wqhq", "PKSA-rdkp-vv9z-mjkg" ]
      }
},
}}}

The legacy `audit.*` keys still work as a fallback, so nothing is broken today. Migrating now removes the warning and avoids breakage when Composer drops the fallback.

WordPress’ Shortcode system has been gradually de-ephasized, but remains in use by many plugins, themes, and Core itself. There are many ways that it is ripe for updating, particularly in harmony with the HTML API.

 - It needs a performance overhaul to replace memory-heavy and inefficient processor.
 - It needs to harmonize with HTML parsing now that WordPress has the ability to understand HTML content.
 - Ambiguities in the grammar needs better explicit documentation. These ambiguities are results of the PCRE patterns which //define// how Shortcodes are parsed.
 - The multiple ways of finding and processing Shortcodes need to be combined into a convenient and single semantic API.

This is a tracking issue to organize the long-term project.

== Notes

=== Parsing is extremely dynamic.

The parsing syntax for Shortcodes is itself dependent on the runtime system when the request is run and when functions like `do_shortcodes()` is called. There may be historic conflation between “only process registered shortcodes” and “only recognize syntax based on registered shortcodes.” This is because //only registered Shortcodes are recognized as Shortcode syntax//.

{{{#!php
<?php

add_shortcode( 's0', fn () => '' );
echo do_shortcodes( 'a[s0]b[s1]c' );
// 'ab[s1]c'
}}}

This dynamism can create surprising behavior, for example, because escaped Shortcodes are not recognized for non-registered Shortcodes. In the following snippet, one //might// expect that `[s0]` should not be rendered because it appears to be part of the content of the non-registered `[s1]` Shortcode, which itself is escaped, meaning that the entire input string would come out unaltered by `do_shortcodes()`.

{{{#!php
<?php

add_shortcode( 's0', fn () => '' );
echo do_shortcodes( '[[s1]a[s0]b[/s1]]' );
// '[[s1]ab[/s1]]'
}}}

This dynamism also implies that one cannot “find” the Shortcodes in an HTML document in isolation. In fact, a given document may have a different set of Shortcodes in it based on who is asking, when, and proverbially what direction the wind is blowing.

=== Parsing only matches positive assertive patterns.

Due to the way that Shortcode handling is based on PCRE patterns and functions, there is no inherent error-handling for malformed Shortcode syntax. Added to this, the matcher is written as a single PCRE pattern with several noteworthy characteristics;

 - Escaping brackets are captured on either side, regardless if they are balanced. Shortcodes which were meant to be escaped can be swallowed because of a missing escaping bracket at close.
 - Whether a Shortcode expects a closing tag is based on whether the closing tag appears in the same document. Non-registered Shortcodes can interact unexpectedly with closing tags, creating them when they were not intended to exist.
 - Rules for parsing switch between affirmative lists (e.g. “a tag name may contain these characters”) and negative lists (e.g. “to parse a tag name, match until these characters appear”) and this leaves room for different code to have different understandings of where Shortcodes exist.
 - Shortcodes without closing tags, or Shortcodes whose closing tags appear far later in a document, cause an undue performance burden during parsing.

{{{#!php
<?php
$input = 'a[s0]b[s1]c[/s0]d[/s1]e';
add_shortcode( 's1', fn ( $attrs, $content ) => "[s1:{$content}]" );
do_shortcodes( $input );
add_shortcode( 's0', fn ( $attrs, $content ) => "[s0:{$content}]" );
do_shortcodes( $input );

//   input: a[s0]b[s1]c[/s0]d[/s1]e
//      s1: a[s0]b[s1:c[/s0]d]e
// s0 + s1: a[s0:b[s1]c]d[/s1]e
}}}


The documentation thankfully notes some of the wishy-washy nature of Shortcode handling.

 - [https://codex.wordpress.org/Shortcode_API Shortcode API]
 - [https://codex.wordpress.org/Shortcode Shortcode]

This helpfully acknowledges that defining behavior is difficult given that most of the existing behaviors are emergency due to the way the PCRE pattern happened to have been written, but it misses out on being helpful in clarifying expectations for how given Shortcode syntax will be handled. It fails to build an intuition on Shortcode handling.

=== Behavior has changed over time.

There have been changes in how Shortcodes are processed and the documentation makes clear that not all existing behaviors are guaranteed. Almost all variation occurs in abnormal cases, where markup is malformed or where there is valid-looking Shortcode syntax for non-registered Shortcodes.

Notably, an attempt was made to only parse Shortcodes within HTML syntax tokens. Unfortunately, at the time this appeared, WordPress had no reliable mechanism for parsing HTML and so a number of extant behaviors remain as emergent from those decisions.

Enforcing a new intentional set of behaviors can clarify and simplify how Shortcodes work in WordPress.

=== Finding potential Shortcode closers first be much more efficient.

Shortcode closing tags are only matched with very specific syntax. Yet, when reaching an opening tag, it’s required to scan, potentially, to the end of the document to find out //if// a closing tag exists. This occurs for every opening Shortcode tag.

However, a quick pass through the document can find a set of candidate Shortcode closing tags, and as opening tags are found, a rapid search through this candidate list can return matching closers. Advancing past a candidate closing Shortcode purges the set of candidates, maintaining search efficiency.

== Tasks

 - [ ] Replace call to `wp_kses_hair_parse()` with HTML API parsing.
 - [ ] Rebuild parser with efficient streaming parser.
 - [ ] Define changes in behavior to normalize processing.

== Related work

 - #50683 Rebuild the Shortcode parser.

The Style Engine keeps a declaration's value twice: once as the value it was given, and once as the CSS it compiles. The two disagree when the value contains a run of whitespace inside a quoted string, because every value is filtered through `wp_strip_all_tags( $value, true )`, whose second argument replaces each run of `\r`, `\n`, `\t` or space with a single space.

== Reproduction ==

A font family whose name contains two spaces, which needs nothing but core:

{{{#!php
<?php
$styles = wp_style_engine_get_styles(
    array( 'typography' => array( 'fontFamily' => '"My  Font", sans-serif' ) )
);

$styles['declarations']['font-family'];  // "My  Font", sans-serif
$styles['css'];                          // font-family:"My Font", sans-serif;
}}}

The serialized CSS no longer names exactly the family that was supplied, so it may match a different family or fall back. Nothing reports that the value was changed.

== Where it happens ==

`WP_Style_Engine_CSS_Declarations::filter_declaration()`:

{{{#!php
<?php
$filtered_value = wp_strip_all_tags( $value, true );
}}}

and in `wp_strip_all_tags()`:

{{{#!php
<?php
if ( $remove_breaks ) {
    $text = preg_replace( '/[\r\n\t ]+/', ' ', $text );
}

return trim( $text );
}}}

The helper's `$remove_breaks` parameter is documented as removing "left over line breaks and white space chars", so this behavior is consistent with its contract. It is unsafe for CSS values where whitespace inside a quoted string is data rather than formatting, and the Style Engine applies it to every declaration it writes.

== How this came up ==

While adding `font-variation-settings` in [https://github.com/WordPress/wordpress-develop/pull/13789 #13789] (Trac #66198). The [https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/typography/opentype/spec/dvaraxisreg OpenType Design-Variation Axis Tag Registry] defines an axis tag as four bytes beginning with a letter, and allows a tag with fewer than four letters or digits to be padded with trailing spaces, so `"abc "` and `"a   "` are valid tags. Accepting them produced this:

{{{
value given:   array( 'a   ' => 12 )
declarations:  font-variation-settings: "a   " 12
compiled CSS:  font-variation-settings: "a " 12
}}}

`"a "` is a different tag from `"a   "`, so the axis is not the one that was asked for.

That pull request now refuses a padded tag. That is a limit rather than a fix: it keeps the Style Engine from writing a tag other than the one requested, and it leaves WordPress unable to carry a space-padded axis tag at all. The tags in ordinary use are four characters, so the practical cost is small, but the reason it was chosen is this ticket.

== Scope ==

This is not specific to typography. Any CSS value whose meaning depends on repeated whitespace inside a quoted string is affected, and the family-name case above needs no new feature to reproduce.

I am not proposing a particular fix here, because the call sits on the path of every declaration the Style Engine emits: narrowing it changes output well beyond typography, and it is part of a sanitization path, so it deserves to be looked at with that in mind rather than patched from inside a feature branch.

Related: Trac #66198, [https://github.com/WordPress/wordpress-develop/pull/13789 wordpress-develop#13789].

When Twenty Fourteen's Featured Content option "Don't display tag on front end" is enabled, the featured tag is still displayed in a post's tag list on the front end (e.g. the tag links under a single post).

=== Steps to Reproduce
1. Activate Twenty Fourteen.
2. Appearance > Customize > Featured Content: set the tag name to `featured` and enable "Don't display tag on front end".
3. Create a post with the tags `featured` and another tag, then publish.
4. View the post on the front end.

=== Expected Results
The `featured` tag is hidden from the post's tag list.

=== Actual Results
The `featured` tag is shown in the post's tag list.

=== Cause
`Featured_Content::hide_the_featured_term()` (filter on `get_the_terms`) removes the matching term with:

{{{
unset( $terms[ $term->term_id ] );
}}}

The array returned by `get_the_terms()` is indexed from 0, not keyed by term ID, so the featured tag stays in the list. Worse, if the post has more tags than the featured tag's ID, a different, unrelated tag is removed instead (e.g. featured tag ID 3 on a post with 4+ tags hides the 4th tag).

The sibling method `hide_featured_term()` (filter on `get_terms`) already uses the loop index correctly:

{{{
unset( $terms[ $order ] );
}}}

=== Proposed Fix
Use the loop index in `hide_the_featured_term()` as well:

{{{
unset( $terms[ $order ] );
}}}

I tested this locally (WordPress 7.2-alpha-63166-src, PHP 8.3.33): with the fix, the featured tag is hidden on the front end when the option is enabled, and still shown when the option is disabled.

Found while testing #64882 (a different issue in the same file). I'll submit a PR.

When using a translated version of WordPress (e.g., fr_FR) with the Classic Editor, the translated strings for the "Save Draft" and "Preview" buttons are longer than in English. This causes the buttons to wrap onto two lines within the #minor-publishing-actions container. 

Due to recent CSS changes applied to buttons, there is currently no vertical margin between them when they stack, causing a visual overlap.

== Steps to reproduce ==
1. Install and activate the Classic Editor plugin.
2. Set the Site Language to French (fr_FR) to ensure the button text is long enough to wrap.
3. Navigate to Articles → Ajouter un article.
4. Observe the "Publier" metabox (specifically the "Enregistrer le brouillon" and "Prévisualiser" buttons).
5. Notice that the buttons are stacked and touch each other without any vertical spacing.

== Expected result ==
There should be a consistent vertical gap between the buttons when they are forced onto multiple lines.

== Possible solution ==
A simple CSS adjustment could be added to handle the spacing when items wrap. For example, applying a bottom margin to the first button's container or utilizing flexbox gap on the parent #minor-publishing-actions:

{{{
#minor-publishing-actions {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: 8px;
    justify-content: space-between;
}

/* Fallback/alternative */
#save-post {
    margin-bottom: 8px;
}
}}}

When a `$path` is passed to `wp_get_global_settings()` or `wp_get_global_styles()` and that path does not exist, both functions return the '''entire''' settings/styles array instead of an empty value.

This makes it impossible for callers to distinguish "this setting is not set" from "here is every setting", so defensive code has to sniff for expected keys and guess.

Originally reported in Gutenberg: https://github.com/WordPress/gutenberg/issues/39657

== Steps to reproduce ==

{{{#!php
<?php
add_action( 'init', function () {
	var_dump( wp_get_global_settings( array( 'layout', 'contentSize' ) ) ); // string, e.g. '645px'
	var_dump( wp_get_global_settings( array( 'foo', 'bar' ) ) );            // entire settings array (12 top-level keys)
	var_dump( wp_get_global_styles( array( 'foo', 'bar' ) ) );              // entire styles array
}, 9999 );
}}}

== Expected ==

`null` when the requested path does not exist.

== Actual ==

The full settings (or styles) array.

== Cause ==

In `src/wp-includes/global-styles-and-settings.php`:

{{{#!php
return _wp_array_get( $settings, $path, $settings ); // wp_get_global_settings()
return _wp_array_get( $styles, $path, $styles );     // wp_get_global_styles()
}}}

The third argument of `_wp_array_get()` is the default returned when the path is not found, and here it is the whole array.

== Proposed fix ==

Return `null` for a missing path (i.e. drop the third argument / pass `null`), while keeping the existing behaviour of returning everything when `$path` is empty:

{{{#!php
if ( empty( $path ) ) {
	return $settings;
}
return _wp_array_get( $settings, $path, null );
}}}

Why `null` rather than an empty array:
 * It is `_wp_array_get()`'s own default for a missing path.
 * It distinguishes "not set" from a setting whose value legitimately ''is'' an empty array.
 * It makes the `?? $fallback` idiom work for callers.

== Backward compatibility ==

This is a behaviour change. Callers of both functions should be audited for any that pass a path that may be absent. In practice, a caller currently receiving the full array for a missing path is already getting an unusable value, so impact should be minimal. A dev note may be warranted.

Also needs unit tests covering: existing path, missing path, and empty path (returns full array).

`wp_nav_menu()` passes the result of `wp_get_nav_menu_items()` to `_wp_menu_item_classes_by_context()` ( [https://github.com/WordPress/wordpress-develop/blob/trunk/src/wp-includes/nav-menu-template.php#L201 GH] ), which assigns properties on every item without checking that the item is an object ( [https://github.com/WordPress/wordpress-develop/blob/trunk/src/wp-includes/nav-menu-template.php#L399 GH] ). The items come through the `wp_get_nav_menu_items` filter, and in the normal case they are all WP_Post objects.

A filter that leaves a `null` in that array causes a PHP fatal error:

{{{
Fatal error: Uncaught Error: Attempt to assign property "current" on null ...
}}}

WordPress should check that each menu item is an object before assigning properties to it.

I have a PR to address this.

I have a bunch of links on my site, running WordPress 7.1.2 using the stock Twenty Seventeen theme. I have added the stock links/blogroll widget to my sidebar, and asked it to display only the link name:

[[Image(https://core.trac.wordpress.org/raw-attachment/ticket/66165/Screenshot%202026-09-23%20at%2010.01.59.webp, alt="Links widget UI", title="")]]

However, it also shows the link rating, so every link has a `0` displayed after it. I don't know what link ratings are, or how they are meant to be used/displayed, but I don't want them.

I found the code responsible in `wp-includes/bookmark-template.php` after line 144, where it checks for the existence of the `show_rating` property. I dumped that `$parsed_args` array and see that `show_rating` is `1`, even though it is disabled in the widget UI. The same is true for the `show_images` and `show_description` properties, which I have also turned off, yet they are both `1` in the data.

In this screen shot, the link name is "Next site", the rating is 0, followed by the `$parsed_args` array as JSON.

[[Image(https://core.trac.wordpress.org/raw-attachment/ticket/66165/Screenshot%202026-09-23%20at%2010.06.54.webp, alt="Links widget data dump", title="")]]

{{{
Next site
