Hi, 
Whenever I run a W3C check on WordPress sites, this error always pops up. How can I avoid it?


{{{
Element style not allowed as child of element body in this context.
<style id="core-block-supports-inline-css">
}}}

`WP_REST_Server::serve_batch_request_v1()` builds each sub-request as

{{{#!php
$parsed_url = wp_parse_url( $args['path'] );

if ( false === $parsed_url ) {
	$requests[] = new WP_Error( 'parse_path_failed', ... );
	continue;
}

$single_request = new WP_REST_Request( $args['method'] ?? 'POST', $parsed_url['path'] );
}}}

`wp_parse_url()` only returns `false` for a URL it cannot parse at all. For a value that parses but has no path component it returns an array **without a `path` key**, e.g.

{{{#!php
wp_parse_url( 'http://host' ); // [ 'scheme' => 'http', 'host' => 'host' ]
wp_parse_url( '?x=1' );        // [ 'query' => 'x=1' ]
}}}

so `$parsed_url['path']` is `null` (with an "Undefined array key" warning) and the sub-request is constructed with `$route = null`.

The sub-request then fails `match_request_to_handler()` as expected, but before core rejects it the dispatch loop still runs

{{{#!php
$result = apply_filters( 'rest_pre_dispatch', null, $this, $clean_request );
}}}

on it. Every `rest_pre_dispatch` listener now receives a request whose `get_route()` returns `null`, although the method is documented as `@return string` and the constructor's `$route` as `string`. Any listener that trusts that contract fatals:

 * WooCommerce 11.1.0: `Automattic\WooCommerce\Utilities\RestApiUtil::attach_lazy_loaded_namespace(): Argument #3 ($rest_route) must be of type string, null given`
 * WP-ImmoMakler 5.68.0: `strpos(): Argument #1 ($haystack) must be of type string, null given`

Without such a plugin the request still logs `Warning: Undefined array key "path"` from `serve_batch_request_v1()`, and the sub-request is rejected with `rest_no_route` (404) instead of the `parse_path_failed` (400) that an unparseable path gets — the two cases are the same thing to the caller and should be reported the same way.

== Steps to reproduce ==

 1. WordPress 7.1, PHP 8.x, WooCommerce active (or any plugin with a typed `rest_pre_dispatch` callback).
 2. Unauthenticated:
{{{
curl -X POST 'https://example.test/?rest_route=/batch/v1' \
     -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \
     -d '{"requests":[{"path":"?x=1"}]}'
}}}
 3. Expected: `207 Multi-Status` with a `parse_path_failed` / `rest_no_route` body for the sub-request.
 4. Actual: `500` with `Uncaught TypeError` from the first `rest_pre_dispatch` listener that type-hints the route.

== Proposed fix ==

Treat a path that parses without a path component the same as one that does not parse at all — it can never match a route anyway (patch attached):

{{{#!diff
--- a/src/wp-includes/rest-api/class-wp-rest-server.php
+++ b/src/wp-includes/rest-api/class-wp-rest-server.php
@@ -1809,7 +1809,7 @@ class WP_REST_Server {
 		foreach ( $batch_request['requests'] as $args ) {
 			$parsed_url = wp_parse_url( $args['path'] );
 
-			if ( false === $parsed_url ) {
+			if ( false === $parsed_url || ! isset( $parsed_url['path'] ) ) {
 				$requests[] = new WP_Error( 'parse_path_failed', __( 'Could not parse the path.' ), array( 'status' => 400 ) );
 
 				continue;
}}}

Verified against 7.1: with the patch, `{"path":"http://host"}`, `{"path":"?x=1"}` and `{"path":"http://:"}` all return `207` with a `parse_path_failed` (400) sub-response; a valid path is unaffected. The `$matches`/`$validation` alignment introduced in 6.9.5 / 7.0.2 is preserved because the error is pushed to `$requests` at the same point the existing `false` case is.

A second, independent hardening would be for `rest_pre_dispatch` not to fire for a sub-request that has already failed matching — but that is a behaviour change for listeners, so the patch above is the minimal fix.

For context on why this surfaced now: `/batch/v1` is currently being probed at scale with malformed `path` values, and the variants that parse without a path component produce the 500 above on plugins that trust `get_route()`'s documented type — we saw it on 20+ unrelated, fully patched 7.1 sites in one night. The batch endpoint itself behaves correctly on those requests; this is purely a robustness issue in how the rejected sub-request is handed to filters.

== Tests ==

The attached patch adds `test_batch_v1_path_without_path_component_is_rejected()` to `Tests_REST_Server` with a data provider for `http://host`, `?x=1` and `#top`. It asserts a `207` batch response whose sub-response is `parse_path_failed` (400), and that `rest_pre_dispatch` did not run for the rejected sub-request.

On unpatched trunk (`7.2-alpha-63166`) all three data sets error out with `Undefined array key "path"` at `class-wp-rest-server.php:1818`; with the patch they pass, and the full `Tests_REST_Server` suite stays green (132 tests).

[45569] / #47489 fixed `convert_smilies()` so that emoji and smilies inside ignored tags (`code`, `pre`, `style`, `script`, `textarea`) are left alone even when the tag has attributes — for example the block editor's Code/Preformatted blocks, which add `class="wp-block-code"` to the `<pre>` tag.

That fix was only applied to `convert_smilies()`. The identical logic in `wp_staticize_emoji()` was never updated, so it still only recognizes an ignored tag when the tag closes immediately after its name:

{{{#!php
// wp_staticize_emoji() — current
if ( '' === $ignore_block_element && preg_match( '/^<(' . $tags_to_ignore . ')>/', $content, $matches ) ) {
}}}

Compare the corrected sibling function:

{{{#!php
// convert_smilies() — fixed in #47489
if ( '' === $ignore_block_element && preg_match( '/^<(' . $tags_to_ignore . ')[^>]*>/', $content, $matches ) ) {
}}}

Because `wp_staticize_emoji()` runs on `the_content_feed`, `comment_text_rss`, and outgoing mail (`wp_staticize_emoji_for_email`), an emoji placed inside a `<pre class="wp-block-code">` or `<code ...>` block is wrongly converted to a static `<img>` in RSS/Atom feeds and email — even though the same content is correctly left untouched on the rendered page thanks to #47489.

== Steps to reproduce ==
1. Create a post with a Code block (or any `<pre class="...">` / `<code ...>`) containing an emoji, e.g. 🙂.
2. View the site feed at `/feed/` (or trigger an email containing the content).
3. Expected: the emoji inside the code/pre block is left as-is.
   Actual: the emoji is replaced with `<img class="wp-smiley" ... />`.

== Proposed fix ==
Apply the same `[^>]*` tolerance to `wp_staticize_emoji()` that `convert_smilies()` already uses. Patch with unit tests attached.

Hi everyone,

I noticed a small contrast issue on the login screen and wanted to report it, along with a proposed fix: happy to hear if I'm missing something or if this has already been discussed elsewhere. I searched Trac before opening this, but didn't find an existing one report, so apologies in advance if this turns out to be a duplicate.

Description: when more than one language is installed, WordPress shows a language switcher button on wp-login.php. Looking at login.css, the rule for this button (`.login .language-switcher .button`) only sets margin-bottom and it doesn't declare its own background, so it relies implicitly on whatever background happens to sit behind it.

This works fine with the default light login background, but if the login screen is customized (via a custom stylesheet enqueued, a white-labeling plugin, or similar) with a dark background or one close to the button's own text color, the button's text becomes very hard to read, since nothing guarantees contrast between the two.

How to reproduce: with WordPress Playground use the following blueprint:

{{{
{
  "landingPage": "/wp-login.php",
  "preferredVersions": { "php": "8.0", "wp": "latest" },
  "steps": [
    {
      "step": "setSiteLanguage",
      "language": "it_IT"
    },
    {
      "step": "writeFile",
      "path": "/wordpress/wp-content/mu-plugins/repro-login-bg.php",
      "data": "<?php add_action('login_enqueue_scripts', function() { echo '<style>body.login{background:#3c3c3c;}</style>'; });"
    }
  ],
  "login": false
}
}}}

Expected: the button should have an explicit background so it stays readable regardless of the page's background.

Actual: the button's background is implicit/transparent and blends into custom login backgrounds.

I've attached simple a patch that adds an explicit background-color to the button rule, reusing a value already used for backgrounds elsewhere in wp-admin.
Would appreciate a second look on whether that's the right value to reuse, or if there's a more appropriate approach for this (e.g. using a dedicated class and so on).

Thanks for taking a look!

`WP_Font_Face::order_src()` picks each URL's format from its file extension:

{{{
$format         = pathinfo( $url, PATHINFO_EXTENSION );
$src[ $format ] = $url;
}}}

For `https://example.org/f/a.woff2?ver=abc123`, `pathinfo()` returns `woff2?ver=abc123`. The method then only keeps the `woff2`, `woff`, `ttf`, `eot` and `otf` keys, so the URL is dropped and the `@font-face` rule is printed without a `src`. There is no notice.

To reproduce (WordPress 7.1, no plugins):

{{{
wp_print_font_faces( array( array( array(
	'font-family' => 'T',
	'src'         => array( 'https://example.org/f/a.woff2?ver=abc123' ),
) ) ) );
}}}

Output:

{{{
@font-face{font-family:T;font-style:normal;font-weight:400;font-display:fallback;}
}}}

Without `?ver=abc123` the same call prints `src:url('https://example.org/f/a.woff2') format('woff2');`.

Fonts from `theme.json` use `file:./` paths with no query string, which may be why this has not shown up. It does affect a plugin that adds a version to its font URLs for cache busting, the usual practice for assets; such a plugin has to print its own `@font-face` rules instead of using `wp_print_font_faces()`.

A possible fix is to read the extension from the URL path only:

{{{
$format = pathinfo( (string) wp_parse_url( $url, PHP_URL_PATH ), PATHINFO_EXTENSION );
}}}

with a dataset case in `tests/phpunit/tests/fonts/font-face/wp-font-face-tests-dataset.php` for a URL with a query string.

Found while building an emoji font fallback; wider context in [https://github.com/WordPress/gutenberg/discussions/83032 Gutenberg discussion #83032]. Related, same class: #66103.

The `mysql` service healthcheck in `docker-compose.yml` pings the database with `-h localhost`. To the MySQL and MariaDB clients, `localhost` means "use the Unix socket". It is the one hostname that does not go over the network: `127.0.0.1` and every other host connect over TCP.

While a cold database volume is initialised, the entrypoint of both images starts a temporary server with `--skip-networking`, on the same socket path:

{{{
# mariadb, docker-entrypoint.sh
"$@" --skip-networking --default-time-zone=SYSTEM --socket="${SOCKET}" ...

# mysql, docker-entrypoint.sh
if ! "$@" --daemonize --skip-networking --default-time-zone=SYSTEM --socket="${SOCKET}"; then
}}}

That temporary server answers the socket ping, but binds no listener on port 3306. The container is therefore reported healthy before it accepts a single connection over the network, and `depends_on: condition: service_healthy` releases the `cli`, `php` and `nginx` containers too early. WP-CLI then fails with:

{{{
Error: Database connection error (2002) Connection refused
}}}

== Measurement ==

Running the resolved healthcheck command in a tight loop against a cold volume, and recording the first probe at which it passes versus the first probe at which port 3306 accepts a connection. Docker's own 5 second sampling interval can step over the window entirely, which is why the check has to be exercised directly:

{{{
engine          healthcheck passed    TCP accepted
mysql 8.4       probe 44              probe 76
mariadb 11.4    probe 40              probe 67
}}}

On MySQL 8.4 the entrypoint stops the temporary server before it starts the final one, so in the middle of that window there is no server running at all, and even the socket stops answering. Docker has already marked the container healthy by then, and it only reverts to unhealthy after `retries` consecutive failures, which at the current settings is 10 probes at a 5 second interval. The container therefore stays marked healthy across a period when `mysqld` is not running and calls will fail.

== Proposed fix ==

Ping over TCP, so the check cannot pass against the temporary server:

{{{
-h 127.0.0.1 --protocol=tcp
}}}

No credentials are required. `mysqladmin ping` and `mariadb-admin ping` exit `0` on `Access denied`, which is the correct result here: the check asserts that the server is reachable, not that the caller can authenticate.

Note that switching from `localhost` to `127.0.0.1` is sufficient as providing an IP address will make the check run using TCP, the `--protocol=tcp` part is redundant and added to make the nature of the check explicit.

Verified against mysql 5.7, mysql 8.4, mariadb 10.3 and mariadb 11.4, covering both branches of the version conditional in the healthcheck. In every case the check no longer passes before the port accepts connections, and `docker compose up -d --wait mysql` reports healthy in 10-11 seconds on a cold volume, well inside the existing 60 second `start_period`.

== Relationship to other tickets ==

#65742 fixed the same symptom in `tools/local-env/scripts/install.js` by retrying `wp config create` until it succeeds ([62938]). That retry works, but it treats one WP-CLI command as the readiness probe and leaves the healthcheck reporting the wrong thing to every other consumer of `depends_on`. This ticket is about the healthcheck predicate itself.

#64579 reports the same symptom as #65742 and is still open; it looks like a duplicate.

#65752 and #65799 both adjusted this healthcheck for unrelated reasons.

== Backporting ==

Every branch back to 4.7 runs its PHPUnit CI through `reusable-phpunit-tests-v1/v2/v3.yml@trunk`, which checks out the caller's branch and runs `npm run env:start` and `npm run env:install` against it. The compose file in effect is the branch's own, so a fix on trunk does not reach them. The same one-line change applies to all of them, in two variants: 4.7 through 6.7 carry the simple form of the healthcheck, 6.8 and trunk carry the version-cased form.

When a post has no manual excerpt, `wp_trim_excerpt()` generates one from post content. Since [59523] (#61074), `the_content` carries a Block Hooks callback at priority 8, `apply_block_hooks_to_content` originally, replaced by `apply_block_hooks_to_content_from_post_object` in [59838] (#61074, #62716), both first released in 6.8.0:

{{{
#!php
// src/wp-includes/default-filters.php:201
add_filter( 'the_content', 'apply_block_hooks_to_content_from_post_object', 8 ); // BEFORE do_blocks().
}}}

`wp_trim_excerpt()` already unhooks `do_blocks` (priority 9, [56560] / #58682) and `wp_filter_content_tags` (priority 12, [55850] / #56588) around its inner `apply_filters( 'the_content' )` call, but the priority-8 Block Hooks callback was never unhooked, so the Block Hooks algorithm still runs during excerpt generation.

=== Why this is a bug ===

1. '''Hooked-block markup leaks into excerpts as plain text.''' `apply_block_hooks_to_content_from_post_object()` wraps delimiter-free content in a temporary `core/freeform` block and then in a `core/post-content` wrapper block (blocks.php:1295-1336) specifically so that `first_child`/`last_child` hooked blocks still have an anchor. The algorithm therefore runs and serializes hooked blocks into the content. They can never render, because `do_blocks` is unhooked, and when `wp_trim_words()` runs `wp_strip_all_tags()` on the result (formatting.php:4126), the `<!-- -->` block delimiters and HTML tags are stripped, so any markup a hooked block carries (e.g. via the public `hooked_block` filter, blocks.php:1049/1120) leaks into the excerpt as plain text.

Measured: a block hooked to `core/post-content` as `last_child`, given `innerHTML` of `<p>LEAKED HOOKED TEXT</p>` via the `hooked_block` filter, turns the excerpt of a post whose content is `<p>Visible text.</p>` from `'Visible text.'` into `'Visible text. LEAKED HOOKED TEXT'`.

2. '''The work is guaranteed waste.''' Even when nothing leaks, the run can never have a visible effect, because `do_blocks` is unhooked, so inserted hooked blocks are discarded. Every excerpt generation pays a `parse_blocks()` traversal, `get_hooked_blocks()`, the `hooked_block_types`/`hooked_block` filters, and a `_wp_ignored_hooked_blocks` post-meta read (blocks.php:1310) for nothing.

3. '''Wrong context.''' The callback resolves its post context via `get_post()` when none is passed (blocks.php:1280-1282), i.e. the current loop post, not the post passed to `wp_trim_excerpt()`. When an excerpt is generated for a different post (widgets, REST, related-posts lists), `_wp_ignored_hooked_blocks` meta is read from the wrong post and the wrong post's hooked-block suppression is applied.

=== Minimal reproduction ===

{{{
#!php
// Register a hooked block, e.g. in a plugin or theme functions.php.
register_block_type(
	'tests/hooked-block',
	array(
		'block_hooks' => array(
			'core/post-content' => 'last_child',
		),
	)
);

// Give the hooked block markup via the public 'hooked_block' filter.
add_filter(
	'hooked_block',
	static function ( $parsed_hooked_block ) {
		$parsed_hooked_block['innerHTML']    = '<p>LEAKED HOOKED TEXT</p>';
		$parsed_hooked_block['innerContent'] = array( '<p>LEAKED HOOKED TEXT</p>' );
		return $parsed_hooked_block;
	}
);
}}}

Then, on the front end (inside the loop so `get_post()` resolves), for a post whose content is `<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Visible text.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->` and which has no manual excerpt:

{{{
#!php
$excerpt = wp_trim_excerpt( '', $post ); // or the_excerpt() in the loop
}}}

Expected: `Visible text.`[[BR]]
Actual: `Visible text. LEAKED HOOKED TEXT`

=== Precedent ===

Core already performs exactly this unhook for this exact callback: `insert_hooked_blocks_into_rest_response()` removes `apply_block_hooks_to_content_from_post_object` from `the_content` around its own inner `apply_filters( 'the_content' )` call and restores it afterwards (blocks.php:1559-1573, the unhook/restore was introduced in [59523] for `apply_block_hooks_to_content` and switched to the current callback in [59838]). The REST API does it to avoid running the algorithm twice; the excerpt case is stronger, because the run can never produce rendered output at all.

=== Proposed fix ===

Mirror the existing `do_blocks`/`wp_filter_content_tags` dance in `wp_trim_excerpt()` (formatting.php:4045-4069): capture `remove_filter( 'the_content', 'apply_block_hooks_to_content_from_post_object', 8 )` before the inner `apply_filters( 'the_content' )`, and re-add it afterwards only if it was actually removed, the same conditional-restore pattern, so a site that deliberately unhooked the callback is not changed.

The patch includes six PHPUnit tests: the callback is unhooked inside the inner `the_content` run, is restored afterwards, is not resurrected when previously unhooked, the Block Hooks algorithm does not run (detected via the `hooked_block_types` filter, which only fires inside the algorithm, blocks.php:1025/1106), hooked-block markup does not leak into the excerpt, and Block Hooks still apply to a subsequent real `the_content` run.

Test sweep: 5283 tests pass across the formatting / post / query / REST-posts / feed groups; PHPCS clean. A negative control (the same reproduction on the code path without the fix) produces the leaked excerpt `'Visible text. LEAKED HOOKED TEXT'`, confirming the leak exists on trunk.

AI assistance: Yes.[[BR]]
Tool(s): Claude Code (orchestration/review) and Devin (implementation).[[BR]]
Used for: root-cause analysis, patch and test authorship; verified against git history (changesets, tickets, line numbers) and a full test sweep; reviewed and taken responsibility for by the contributor.

An XML-RPC wp.newPost request with a terms_names entry that is an array instead of a string ends in a fatal error:

{{{
curl -i -H 'Content-Type: text/xml' http://localhost:8889/xmlrpc.php \
--data '<?xml version="1.0"?><methodCall><methodName>wp.newPost</methodName><params><param><value><int>1</int></value></param><param><value><string>admin</string></value></param><param><value><string>password</string></value></param><param><value><struct><member><name>post_title</name><value><string>Nested term name</string></value></member><member><name>terms_names</name><value><struct><member><name>post_tag</name><value><array><data><value><array><data><value><string>foo</string></value></data></array></value></data></array></value></member></struct></value></member></struct></value></param></params></methodCall>'
}}}


The terms_names loop in `_insert_post()` does not check the type of each term name. `get_term_by( 'name', $array, $taxonomy )` casts the value to a string and returns false, so the code goes on to create the term:

{{{#!php
<?php
$term_info = wp_insert_term( $term_name, $taxonomy );
}}}

`wp_insert_term()` then runs `'' === trim( $term )`. That causes PHP to throw a TypeError for an array, so instead of an XML-RPC fault the client gets an HTTP 500.

Two things need to change:

1. `wp_insert_term()` should return the existing `empty_term_name` WP_Error for a non-scalar `$term`. This covers every caller, including wp.newTerm, the REST terms controller, and plugins. Numeric term names keep working as they do today.
2. The terms_names loop in `_insert_post()` should reject anything that is not a string with an `IXR_Error`, matching the `Invalid term ID.` check for terms by ID right above it.


I will have a PR to address both of those items.

`wp_get_nav_menu_items()` short-circuits before running its item query whenever the menu term's `count` is 0:

{{{#!php
// src/wp-includes/nav-menu.php
$args = wp_parse_args( $args, $defaults );
if ( $menu->count > 0 ) {
	$items = get_posts( $args );
} else {
	$items = array();
}
}}}

`$menu->count` is not a safe proxy for "no items match these args":

 * '''It counts published items only.''' `_update_post_term_count()` counts posts with `post_status = 'publish'` (the default of the `update_post_term_count_statuses` filter), so a menu whose items are all `draft` or `trash` reports `count = 0` even though a caller querying `'draft'`, `'any'`, `'publish,draft'`, or `'trash'` has matching items.
 * '''It can be stale.''' `wp_defer_term_counting( true )` leaves the count at 0 while items are already attached, and `wp_get_nav_menu_object()` returns a passed `WP_Term` object verbatim, so a caller holding a term snapshot taken before items were added is gated as well.

`post_status` is a documented argument of `wp_get_nav_menu_items()`, so all of the following return `array()` while `get_posts()` with the same arguments returns real items:

 * a draft-only menu queried with `post_status` `'draft'`, `'any'`, or `'publish,draft'`;
 * a trash-only menu queried with `'trash'` (trashing the last published item returns the count to 0);
 * a published item attached while term counting is deferred — hidden even from the default `'publish'` query;
 * any item hidden behind a stale `WP_Term` snapshot passed as `$menu`.

== In-core consequences ==

 * '''Colliding `menu_order`.''' `wp_update_nav_menu_item()` positions a new item by querying `'publish,draft'` items and using `1 +` the last item's `menu_order` (`src/wp-includes/nav-menu.php`, ~line 464). On a menu with no published items — e.g. one being assembled as drafts — the gate empties the list, so the new item gets `menu_order = 0`; every subsequent item collides at position 0 instead of being appended.
 * '''Duplicate auto-added pages.''' `_wp_auto_add_pages_to_menu()` queries the `'publish,draft'` item list to skip pages already in the menu (~line 1164). On a menu with no published items the check sees an empty list and inserts a second item for the same page.

Other in-core callers requesting non-default statuses are affected the same way: `wp_get_nav_menu_to_edit()` queries `'any'` and `wp_nav_menu_update_menu_items()` queries `'draft,publish'` (`src/wp-admin/includes/nav-menu.php`, ~lines 1266 and 1409), so a draft-only menu lists empty on the edit screen and its items can't be updated or deleted there — the save path can't see them either.

== Steps to reproduce ==

{{{#!php
$menu_id = wp_create_nav_menu( 'example' );
$menu    = wp_get_nav_menu_object( $menu_id );

$item_id = wp_update_nav_menu_item(
	$menu_id,
	0,
	array(
		'menu-item-type'   => 'custom',
		'menu-item-title'  => 'Example',
		'menu-item-url'    => 'https://example.org/',
		'menu-item-status' => 'draft',
	)
);

// Returns array() — the gate short-circuits because $menu->count is 0 (drafts are not counted):
$via_function = wp_get_nav_menu_items( $menu_id, array( 'post_status' => 'draft' ) );

// Returns array( $item_id ) — the underlying query does find the item:
$via_query = wp_list_pluck(
	get_posts(
		array(
			'post_type'   => 'nav_menu_item',
			'post_status' => 'draft',
			'nopaging'    => true,
			'tax_query'   => array(
				array(
					'taxonomy' => 'nav_menu',
					'field'    => 'term_taxonomy_id',
					'terms'    => $menu->term_taxonomy_id,
				),
			),
		)
	),
	'ID'
);
}}}

== Root cause ==

The gate was introduced in [53010] (git `556492ad88`) for #55372, which replaced `get_objects_in_term()` + `$args['include']` with a `tax_query` to save one query. The previous `! empty( $items )` check tested whether any objects were attached to the term at all — it was status-agnostic and reflected actual attachments. The replacement `$menu->count > 0` tests the published count only and can additionally be stale, so the gate now fires in cases the old check never hit.

== Proposed fix ==

Remove the short-circuit and always run `get_posts( $args )` — restoring the pre-[53010] approach of letting the query, not the term count, decide whether matching items exist. `get_posts()` already returns `array()` for a genuinely empty menu.

A narrower alternative — keeping the gate only for the default `post_status === 'publish'` query — was considered and rejected: deferred term counting and stale `WP_Term` snapshots both leave `count = 0` while published items exist, so a publish-only gate would still return a wrong `array()` in precisely the cases this ticket covers.

Performance impact of removing the gate (measured in a review sweep of the attached patch):

 * Warm caches, and sites with a persistent object cache: '''zero additional queries''' after a one-time cache miss per menu — the query result, including the empty result, is cached in the `post-queries` group. (That result cache did not yet exist when the gate was added in 6.0.)
 * A site with no object cache: one additional indexed `SELECT` per empty rendered menu per request — it joins `term_relationships` and filters on `term_taxonomy_id` plus the `type_status_date` index, returning 0 rows.
 * Non-empty menus: identical — the gate already passed through to the same query.

== Tests ==

The patch adds 7 regression cases to `Tests_Post_Nav_Menu`: draft-only menus queried with `'draft'`, `'any'`, and `'publish,draft'`; `menu_order` positioning of a second draft item; deferred term counting; a stale `WP_Term` snapshot; and a trash-only menu queried with `'trash'`. Each fails before the change and passes after.

In the same review sweep, 762 existing tests across the menu, customize, REST menus, ajax nav-menus, and related groups passed with the change applied, and the `_wp_auto_add_pages_to_menu()` duplicate insert described above was reproduced under the old code and shown to be gone after it.

== Notes ==

 * A GitHub pull request for code review will be linked shortly.
 * AI assistance was used in preparing the patch and is disclosed in the PR per the [https://make.wordpress.org/ai/handbook/ai-guidelines/ WordPress AI guidelines].

Introduce an optional `$blog_id` argument to `add_action()`, `add_filter()`, `has_action()`, `has_filter()`, `remove_action()`, and `remove_filter()`.

The new argument would allow a developer to register a callback for an explicitly selected site and let the hook dispatcher skip that registration when the current site does not match. Existing registrations would remain global by default. Lookup and removal would accept an optional site selector, following the existing distinction between an unspecified constraint and a specific constraint.

I ran into this while working on a Multisite project where individual sites could be connected to different external services, such as separate search indexes.

A callback registered while site A is current remains registered when another component calls `switch_to_blog()` and performs an operation on site B. As a result, a callback bound to site A's service can receive an event that happened while site B was current.

This ticket proposes allowing an action or filter registration to declare that it belongs to a particular site.

== Problem and motivation ==

In Multisite, `switch_to_blog()` changes the current site data context, but previously loaded plugin and theme code remains loaded. Callbacks registered before the switch therefore remain active after it.

This is documented behavior and is useful for network-wide functionality. It becomes a problem when a callback belongs to a site-specific service instance, for example:

 * an external search index;
 * an integration account;
 * a notification destination;
 * a repository or API client configured for one site.

Consider a `deleted_post` callback connected to site A's search index. If another component switches to site B and deletes a post there, the callback can incorrectly remove an entry from site A's index. Numeric post IDs are not unique across a Multisite network, so the site context is essential.

The same issue applies to filters. A filter associated with site A may provide configuration or modify a value while site B is current.

This can currently be handled with a guard inside every callback:

{{{#!php
$blog_id = get_current_blog_id();

add_action(
	'deleted_post',
	static function ( $post_id, $post ) use ( $blog_id ) {
		if ( get_current_blog_id() !== $blog_id ) {
			return;
		}

		// Update the service associated with this site.
	},
	10,
	2
);
}}}

Once this behavior is known, the workaround is small: keep the guard in each callback, or centralize it in a lightweight registration wrapper or dispatcher. The latter has to be created or adopted by each project and used consistently.

The harder part is discoverability. Nothing at the point of `add_action()` or `add_filter()` indicates that a callback intended for one site will remain eligible after unrelated code switches to another site. The switch may happen in a different plugin or subsystem, so the author of the callback may not know that a guard is required, and a later callback or refactor can easily omit it.

Such an omission usually does not fail immediately. The callback receives valid-looking arguments while the wrong site is current, and the defect may only become visible after an incorrect deletion from an external index, a notification sent through the wrong account, or another cross-site side effect.

An explicit site argument addresses both sides of this problem. It makes the callback's expected execution context visible at the point of registration, and it moves enforcement of that declared constraint to `WP_Hook`, which already decides whether and when the callback is invoked. The developer remains responsible for selecting the intended site; the dispatcher becomes responsible for not invoking that registration while another site is current. This places enforcement in the dispatch layer instead of requiring every business callback to remember the same implicit Multisite invariant. It does not ask Core to switch or repair the current context.

== Minimal example of the current behavior ==

The following synthetic action isolates hook dispatch from post operations or other plugin behavior:

{{{#!php
$origin_blog_id = get_current_blog_id();
$other_blog_id  = 7; // An existing, different site.
$observed       = array();

$callback = static function () use ( &$observed ) {
	$observed[] = get_current_blog_id();
};

add_action( 'example_blog_scope_probe', $callback, 10, 0 );

do_action( 'example_blog_scope_probe' );

switch_to_blog( $other_blog_id );

try {
	do_action( 'example_blog_scope_probe' );
} finally {
	restore_current_blog();
	remove_action( 'example_blog_scope_probe', $callback, 10 );
}
}}}

With the current API, `$observed` contains both `$origin_blog_id` and `$other_blog_id`.

== Proposed direction ==

Add an optional site selector to action and filter registration:

{{{#!php
add_action(
	$hook_name,
	$callback,
	$priority = 10,
	$accepted_args = 1,
	$blog_id = null
);

add_filter(
	$hook_name,
	$callback,
	$priority = 10,
	$accepted_args = 1,
	$blog_id = null
);
}}}

These are signature sketches and do not propose native PHP parameter types.

Existing calls without `$blog_id` would retain their current global behavior. A registration with an explicit site ID would be eligible only while that site is current.

The earlier example could therefore register its callback as:

{{{#!php
add_action(
	'example_blog_scope_probe',
	$callback,
	10,
	0,
	$origin_blog_id
);
}}}

The action would still fire after switching to the other site, but this particular registration would be skipped.

The dispatcher should evaluate the current site when the callback is about to run. It should not call `switch_to_blog()`, load or unload plugins, or infer a site from the callback arguments.

For an initial implementation, I suggest considering a single positive site ID only. Support for multiple site IDs could be discussed separately if there is sufficient need.

== Registration lifecycle ==

If site scope becomes part of a registration, `has_action()`, `has_filter()`, `remove_action()`, and `remove_filter()` will also need a consistent way to address that scope.

The exact API should be decided during review. In particular:

 * whether the scope becomes part of registration identity;
 * how lookup and removal distinguish a specific site, a global registration, and all scopes;
 * whether global and site-specific registrations of the same callback may coexist;
 * what should happen when the same callback and priority are registered for different sites.

I do not think these semantics should be fixed before there is agreement that site-aware registration belongs in the native hooks API.

== Related Core discussions ==

#14941 describes the underlying Multisite behavior: callbacks from the original site's plugins remain active after `switch_to_blog()`. That ticket was closed because switching sites is not intended to load or unload PHP code, and plugins are expected to check the current site themselves.

This proposal does not change that behavior. It would provide an opt-in way for a plugin to declare the check as part of its subscription.

#49518 introduced text-domain-specific translation filters in addition to the existing general filters. It is a related design pattern: developers can subscribe to a narrower set of relevant events instead of handling every invocation of a general hook and checking the context inside the callback. It is not a direct precedent for storing site metadata in `WP_Hook`.

#64186 added priority-aware lookup to `has_action()` and `has_filter()` for consistency with registration and removal. It is relevant if site scope is treated as another registration attribute that must be handled across the hook lifecycle.

== Compatibility and performance ==

The default path for existing registrations must remain unchanged in behavior.

The main compatibility concerns appear to be:

 * the public structure of `WP_Hook::$callbacks`;
 * preinitialized hook arrays;
 * wrappers that currently pass surplus positional arguments;
 * code that temporarily removes and later restores callbacks;
 * registration changes during active or nested dispatch.

There is also a performance concern. Hook dispatch is a hot path, and adding a site check to the central execution loop may affect requests that do not use scoped registrations.

An implementation should therefore benchmark at least:

 * existing global-only registrations;
 * single-site and Multisite requests;
 * mixed global and site-specific registrations;
 * native scoping compared with an equivalent callback-level guard.

This proposal does not assume that native scoping is faster. The performance cost needs to be measured against the API and correctness benefits.

== Expected implementation coverage ==

If the general direction is accepted, an initial patch should verify:

 * unchanged behavior for registrations that do not specify a site;
 * matching and non-matching site registrations for both actions and filters;
 * switching and restoring between sites;
 * nested hook dispatch;
 * lookup and selective removal;
 * callback ordering and mutation during dispatch;
 * the `all` hook path and reference-array variants;
 * preinitialized hooks and early bootstrap behavior.

The detailed test matrix will depend on the registration identity and lookup/removal semantics chosen during review.

== Limits of the proposal ==

Site scoping would only guarantee that a registration runs while its declared site is current.

It would not:

 * determine the site from which a callback argument originated;
 * correct an incorrectly restored site context;
 * load or unload plugins when switching sites;
 * provide authorization;
 * persist registrations between requests;
 * support arbitrary context predicates.

== Feedback requested ==

The main questions are:

 1. Does Core consider site-scoped subscriptions useful enough to belong in the native hooks API, given that callback guards and wrappers already work?
 1. Is an optional `$blog_id` on the existing registration functions an appropriate API, or would separate helper functions be preferable?
 1. Should scope become part of registration identity?
 1. How should global and site-specific registrations be represented in lookup and removal?
 1. Is it preferable to begin with scalar site IDs only?

The intended result is a native and inspectable way to associate a callback with one Multisite site while preserving the existing global registration behavior by default.

Site owners can use `db-error.php` to show a custom error page when the database is unavailable. This replaces the usual message shown with `wp_die()`.

`wp_die()` contains the following code:

{{{#!php
header( "Content-Type: text/html; charset={$parsed_args['charset']}" );
status_header( $parsed_args['response'] );
nocache_headers();
}}}


However, this code isn't present when using `db-error.php`. 

I propose that when using a drop-in error page, WordPress should output status and no-cache headers by default. 

A client site that I work on recently had 1 minute of database downtime, but the error page continued to be shown for several hours. This is because it had been cached by Varnish. It is unintuitive, and not clearly documented, that a developer would need to send the status headers themselves when using `db-error.php`.

The same likely applies to the `maintenance.php` dropin.

== Summary ==

If a shortcode (or any `the_content` callback) calls `apply_filters( 'the_content', ... )` on a block post, WordPress ends up with `wpautop` registered at priority -1 for the rest of the request. Every post rendered after that point on the same page is run through `wpautop` twice: once on the raw block markup and once on the rendered blocks. The visible result is stray `</p>` tags, empty paragraphs, and `<br />` after hidden inputs in things like Query Loop > Post Content, reusable blocks, and form plugin blocks that appear later on the page.

The post with the nesting shortcode looks fine. The damage lands in unrelated content further down the page. In my case it showed up as broken form markup in a reusable block below the article, and it took a while to trace back to a table of contents shortcode in the post body.

This is a follow-up to #45495 / r45139, which fixed nested `the_content` calls that start ''inside'' `do_blocks()`. This ticket covers a different ordering: with the default hook priorities, a shortcode starts a nested `the_content` run at priority 11, before the pending `_restore_wpautop_hook` callback at that same priority.

Real-world trigger: Easy Table of Contents ([https://github.com/ahmedkaludi/easy-table-of-contents/issues/982 easy-table-of-contents#982]).

== Steps to reproduce ==

No plugins or theme needed. Save this as `repro.php` and run `wp eval-file repro.php --skip-plugins --skip-themes`:

{{{
#!php
<?php
// Any callback that runs the_content while the_content is already running.
add_shortcode( 'nested', function () {
	return apply_filters( 'the_content', 'Nested text.' );
} );

$post_a = "<!-- wp:paragraph -->\n<p>[nested]</p>\n<!-- /wp:paragraph -->";
$post_b = "<!-- wp:html -->\n<form action=\"/\">\n<input type=\"hidden\" name=\"a\" value=\"1\">\n<input type=\"hidden\" name=\"b\" value=\"2\">\n<button>Send</button>\n</form>\n<!-- /wp:html -->";

echo "wpautop priority before: ", var_export( has_filter( 'the_content', 'wpautop' ), true ), "\n";
echo "Post B before:\n", apply_filters( 'the_content', $post_b ), "\n";

apply_filters( 'the_content', $post_a );

echo "wpautop priority after:  ", var_export( has_filter( 'the_content', 'wpautop' ), true ), "\n";
echo "Post B after:\n", apply_filters( 'the_content', $post_b ), "\n";
}}}

Output on 7.1:

{{{
wpautop priority before: 10
Post B before:

<form action="/">
<input type="hidden" name="a" value="1">
<input type="hidden" name="b" value="2">
<button>Send</button>
</form>

wpautop priority after:  -1
Post B after:
</p>
<form action="/">
<input type="hidden" name="a" value="1"><br />
<input type="hidden" name="b" value="2"><br />
<button>Send</button><br />
</form></p>
}}}

Same content, filtered before and after post A. The only difference is that post A was rendered in between.

To see it in a browser on stock Twenty Twenty-Five: put `[nested]` in post A, an HTML block like `$post_b` in post B, and add a Query Loop > Post Content block showing post B to the `single` template. View post A.

== Why it happens ==

1. `do_blocks()` (priority 9) sees blocks in post A, so it removes `wpautop` from 10 and adds `_restore_wpautop_hook` at 11.
2. At priority 11, `WP_Hook` loops over `do_shortcode` and then `_restore_wpautop_hook`. PHP `foreach` iterates a copy of the array, so removing callbacks during the loop does not change what it visits.
3. `do_shortcode` runs `[nested]`, which starts an inner `the_content` run. The inner run reaches priority 11 and calls `_restore_wpautop_hook`, which puts `wpautop` back at 10 and removes itself.
4. Back in the outer loop, `_restore_wpautop_hook` is called a second time from the stale copy. `has_filter( 'the_content', '_restore_wpautop_hook' )` now returns `false`, so `$current_priority - 1` evaluates to `-1`, and `wpautop` is added at priority -1.
5. From then on, every `the_content` run autops twice: at -1 on raw block markup, and at 10 because `do_blocks()` only removes the first `wpautop` it finds (the -1 copy) and leaves the 10 copy in place.

r45139 does not help here because the nested run does not start inside `do_blocks()`; it starts from a sibling callback after the restore hook is already queued.

== Proposed fix ==

Make `_restore_wpautop_hook()` a no-op when it is no longer registered, which means an inner run already restored `wpautop`:

{{{
#!diff
--- a/src/wp-includes/blocks.php
+++ b/src/wp-includes/blocks.php
@@ function _restore_wpautop_hook( $content ) {
 	$current_priority = has_filter( 'the_content', '_restore_wpautop_hook' );
 
+	if ( false === $current_priority ) {
+		return $content;
+	}
+
 	add_filter( 'the_content', 'wpautop', $current_priority - 1 );
 	remove_filter( 'the_content', '_restore_wpautop_hook', $current_priority );
 
 	return $content;
 }
}}}

With this guard the script above prints priority 10 both times and post B renders identically. Verified on a 7.1 site and in a standalone `WP_Hook` simulation.

[https://github.com/WordPress/wordpress-develop/pull/13427 PR #13427] contains the guard and regression tests covering nested plain-text and block content, restoration of the original `wpautop` registration, and subsequent block and classic content formatting.

See file `wp-admin/includes/class-wp-debug-data.php` line 571 and following

{{{
// Check if a robots.txt file exists.
}}}

This code prevents `robots.txt` from being determined correctly, if the installation is located in a subdirectory. 
Site health reports: 'Your site is using the dynamic robots.txt file which is generated by WordPress.' This is not correct.

A `robots.txt` file should be located in `$_SERVER["DOCUMENT_ROOT"]` and, in this case, it cannot be handled by WordPress. `get_home_path()` and `$_SERVER["DOCUMENT_ROOT"]` return different values.


How about this code:

	{{{#!php
<?php
        $site       = wp_parse_url( get_home_url() );
	$serverroot = $site['host'];
	if ( isset( $site['path'] ) ) {
            // cannot handled by WP
        }
}}}

The user should provide a `robots.txt` in `$_SERVER[“DOCUMENT_ROOT”]` or relevant directives in `$_SERVER[“DOCUMENT_ROOT”]/.htaccess`.

Follow-up to #63017

There are three constants for enabling PHP-based compression, which are not used anymore:

{{{
define( ‘COMPRESS_CSS’, true );
define( ‘COMPRESS_SCRIPTS’, true );
define( ‘ENFORCE_GZIP’, true )
}}}

Therefore, we should also remove them from the Site Health screen or maybe start with marking them as ''deprecated''.

It looks like we are not showing `ENFORCE_GZIP`, but the other two:

https://github.com/WordPress/WordPress/blob/de3286bd085400ca657d59c8c4e76632bd5591a8/wp-admin/includes/class-wp-debug-data.php#L1519-L1535

We could **not** show them if the constant is ''undefined''.

If the constant ''is'' defined, we could show the value and add a notice that this doesn't do anything anymore.

The `'include'` key of the `$args` parameter is documented as

{{{#!php
@type array        $include      Comma-separated list of page IDs to include. Default empty
}}}

See the [https://developer.wordpress.org/reference/functions/wp_list_pages/ function reference].

While the documented type is `array`, the description says "Comma-separated list of page IDs" which would imply `string`.

`$args['include']` is neither expected to be a comma separated list nor does it have an empty default value. It is expected to be an array of page IDs as correctly indicated by the documented type `array`. `wp_list_pages()` does not handle `$args['include']` at all. It is passed through to `get_pages()` which expects `$args['include']` to be `int[]` defaulting to an empty array and describes it as "Array of page IDs to include.".

In contrast, `$args['exclude']` is expected to be a comma-separated list of page IDs, defaults to an empty string, and is documented accordingly.

After https://github.com/WordPress/wordpress-develop/pull/13270 I've been thinking about the situation with return values from a cache get.

`wp_cache_get()` returns whatever the cache backend holds. In most cases a return type is assumed ( or does minimal insufficient checking ), and when a backend returns something else we run into a fatal error situation.  We've already seen this happen.

I think WordPress core could do a better job of making this easier for developers with a new function that validates the return value type.  After considering different approaches, here is where I am at:

{{{#!php
<?php
function wp_cache_get_valid( $key, $group, $type, $callback = '', bool $force = false, bool $delete_invalid = false, ?bool &$valid = null ) {}
}}}

My aim is to keep simple cases simple, while having enough flexibility to address more complex cases.  For example, this could tighten up `wp_count_posts()` in https://github.com/WordPress/wordpress-develop/blob/7.1/src/wp-includes/post.php#L3464-L3541 -

{{{#!php
	$counts = wp_cache_get( $cache_key, 'counts' );
	if ( false !== $counts ) {
		// We may have cached this before every status was registered.
		foreach ( get_post_stati() as $status ) {
			if ( ! isset( $counts->{$status} ) ) {
				$counts->{$status} = 0;
			}
		}
}}}

which expects an object, but currently doesn't validate that.  With the proposed function it would be a one line change:

{{{#!php
	$counts = wp_cache_get_valid( $cache_key, 'counts', 'object' );
	if ( false !== $counts ) {
}}}

I've got a PR coming that will expand on the details.

This is the standard WordPress `wp-config.php` template, mostly unmodified — but there are a few real issues:

1. Placeholder credentials never filled in
{{{#!php
define( 'DB_NAME', 'database_name_here' );
define( 'DB_USER', 'username_here' );
define( 'DB_PASSWORD', 'password_here' );
}}}

These are still the sample values. As-is, this file will fail to connect to any real database. This needs to be replaced with actual credentials before the site will work.

2. Placeholder secret keys/salts — a real security problem
{{{#!php
define( 'AUTH_KEY',         'put your unique phrase here' );
...
define( 'NONCE_SALT',       'put your unique phrase here' );
}}}

These are meant to be long, random, unique strings. Leaving them as literal placeholder text means:
- Session/auth cookies are far more predictable/forgeable.
- If this exact file is ever reused across sites (or checked into a public repo), all sites share the same "secret," making cookie forgery trivial.

Generate real values from the [https://api.wordpress.org/secret-key/1.1/salt/ WordPress.org secret-key service] or via `wp config shuffle-salts` (WP-CLI).

3. `CONCATENATE_SCRIPTS` defined too early, and questionable value
{{{#!php
define( 'CONCATENATE_SCRIPTS', false );
}}}

This is placed *before* the file's docblock/header and before `ABSPATH` is even set up — it works functionally since `define()` doesn't care about surrounding comments, but it's an unusual/fragile spot to put a custom constant (normally these go in the "Add any custom values" section below). Setting it to `false` also disables JS/CSS concatenation in the admin, which hurts admin dashboard performance — this is usually a temporary debugging aid, not something you want permanently in production.

4. `WP_DEBUG` is `false` with no companion debug-logging setup
Not a bug per se, but worth noting: if this is a dev/staging environment, you'd typically want:
{{{#!php
define( 'WP_DEBUG', true );
define( 'WP_DEBUG_LOG', true );
define( 'WP_DEBUG_DISPLAY', false );
}}}

so errors are logged to a file instead of either being silently swallowed or (worse) displayed on a live site.

5. No `DISALLOW_FILE_EDIT` / hardening constants**
Not a "bug," but commonly paired with this file for production security (e.g., disabling the plugin/theme file editor, forcing SSL for admin/login).

Summary: The file is structurally correct WordPress boilerplate, but it is not safe to deploy as-is — the unfilled `DB_*` credentials will break the DB connection, and the unfilled `AUTH_KEY`/`*_SALT` placeholders are a genuine security weakness, not just a style nit.

On the source of `WP_List_Table` ( https://github.com/WordPress/wordpress-develop/blob/3f5fdc537810c3b11f8084f3d35398edad5f8f35/src/wp-admin/includes/class-wp-list-table.php#L1383 ), the hidden columns can be set to `_column_headers` property.

The hidden columns can hide properly on another columns except on the **check box column**.

From my example ( https://github.com/Rundiz-WP/demo-listtable-ajax/blob/f6691a114414cd16d0911518e55d8592b91b23cf/App/DB/CustomersListTableHideCb.php#L238-L242 ), I was set the hidden column as check box column. 
{{{#!php
<?php
$this->_column_headers = [
    $this->get_columns(),
    ['cb'],
    $this->get_sortable_columns(),
];
}}}

By default, WordPress did not hide this column (as seen in the method `single_row_columns()` https://github.com/WordPress/wordpress-develop/blob/3f5fdc537810c3b11f8084f3d35398edad5f8f35/src/wp-admin/includes/class-wp-list-table.php#L1824-L1827 )
This happens on WP 7.0, 7.1 and not sure that the older versions too or not.

I tried to override the method `single_row_column` and it seems to work but next problem is, it (check box column) is not hidden properly on small screen of WordPress 7.0- (or older).

I am not sure is this the main purpose of `WP_List_Table` class to always display checkbox column. Or not?  
If it is, then this ticket will be end here.

(If this is main purpose of this column, it still weird that checkbox column only hide on `<thead>` and `<tfoot>` except `<tbody>`.)

----

Step to re-produce:
1. Create some simple extended of list table class with hidden column as `cb`.
2. Create display page to display the list table.
3. Open this list table page.

Expect: List table hide the column checkbox completely.
Actual: Only checkbox column header, footer is hide. Not in `<tbody>`.

**Fix 1**: override `single_row_columns()` method.

{{{
    protected function single_row_columns( $item ) 
    {
        global $wp_version;
        if (version_compare($wp_version, '7.1', '>=')) {
            $wp7_1 = true;
        } else {
            $wp7_1 = false;
        }


        list($columns, $hidden, $sortable, $primary) = $this->get_column_info();


        foreach ($columns as $column_name => $column_display_name) {
            $classes = "$column_name column-$column_name";
            if ($primary === $column_name) {
                $classes .= ' has-row-actions column-primary';
            }


            if (in_array($column_name, $hidden, true)) {
                $classes .= ' hidden';
            }


            /*
             * Comments column uses HTML in the display name with screen reader text.
             * Strip tags to get closer to a user-friendly string.
             */
            $data = 'data-colname="' . esc_attr(wp_strip_all_tags($column_display_name)) . '"';


            $attributes = "class='$classes' $data";


            if ('cb' === $column_name) {
                $tag = (true === $wp7_1 ? 'td' : 'th');
                $cb_attributes = (false === $wp7_1 ? ' scope="row"' : '');
                $cb_classes = 'check-column';
                if (in_array($column_name, $hidden, true)) {
                    $cb_classes .= ' hidden demo-listtable-ajax-hidecb-column';
                }
                echo '<' . $tag . $cb_attributes . ' class="' . esc_attr($cb_classes) . '">';
                echo $this->column_cb($item);
                echo '</' . $tag . '>';
            } elseif (method_exists($this, '_column_' . $column_name)) {
                echo call_user_func(
                    array($this, '_column_' . $column_name),
                    $item,
                    $classes,
                    $data,
                    $primary
                );
            } else {
                if (true === $wp7_1) {
                    $is_primary = ($primary === $column_name);
                    $tag = $is_primary ? 'th' : 'td';
                    $scope = $is_primary ? ' scope="row"' : '';


                    $aria_label = '';
                    if ($is_primary) {
                        $label = $this->get_primary_column_aria_label($item);
                        if ('' !== $label) {
                            $aria_label = ' aria-label="' . esc_attr($label) . '"';
                        }
                    }
                } else {
                    $aria_label = '';
                    $scope = '';
                    $tag = 'td';
                }


                echo "<$tag $attributes$scope$aria_label>";


                if (method_exists($this, 'column_' . $column_name)) {
                    echo call_user_func(array($this, 'column_' . $column_name), $item);
                } else {
                    echo $this->column_default($item, $column_name);
                }


                echo $this->handle_row_actions($item, $column_name, $primary);
                echo "</$tag>";
            }
        }// endforeach;
    }// single_row_columns
}}}
**Result**: Only fixed on WordPress 7.1+ or newer. But WordPress 7.0.x or older still has a problem on small screen that checkbox column is not hide.

**Fix2**: Enqueue this CSS for WordPress 7.0.x or older.

{{{
@media (max-width: 782px) {
    .demo-listtable-ajax-hidecb-column {
        display: none !important;/* On small screen with hidden check box column, the column not really hidden due to `display: table-cell;` by WP core. */
    }
}
}}}
**Result**: The checkbox column is now completely hide on small and large screen, based on WordPress 7.0.x.

Pressing a meta box's '''Move up''' / '''Move down''' arrow destroys any TinyMCE editor rendered inside that box with `wp_editor()`. The editor goes blank on screen, its content becomes unreachable, and switching Visual/Text afterwards throws an uncaught `TypeError`.

This is core-only. It needs no plugins and does not depend on the block editor.

== Cause ==

`postboxes.handleOrderBetweenSortables()` relocates the box across containers with `detach()` + `appendTo()`/`prependTo()` — see `wp-admin/js/postbox.js` lines 181–189. Re-inserting a subtree that contains an `<iframe>` makes the browser give that iframe a brand-new, empty document. TinyMCE keeps a reference to the discarded one, so `editor.getDoc()` and `iframe.contentDocument` are no longer the same object, and every subsequent operation acts on a document that is not on screen.

`handleOrder()` also reorders within a single container using `.before()` and `.after()` (lines 129 and 143). That is the same re-insertion, so it should break the same way — I have '''not''' tested that path.

Note the guard at line 173:

{{{
// Return if there's only one visible sortables area, e.g. in the block editor page.
}}}

On a current block editor screen there are three visible sortables areas (`normal-sortables`, `advanced-sortables`, `side-sortables`), so the guard does not fire and a lone meta box hops containers on the first arrow press.

== Steps to reproduce ==

 1. Drop this in `wp-content/mu-plugins/`:

{{{#!php
<?php
add_action( 'add_meta_boxes', function () {
	add_meta_box( 'my-custom-metabox', 'Custom Meta', 'demo_metabox_cb' );
} );

function demo_metabox_cb( $post ) {
	wp_editor( get_post_meta( $post->ID, 'my-custom-meta', true ), 'my-custom-meta' );
}
}}}

 2. Open '''Posts → Add New''', expand the Meta Boxes panel, and type some text into the Custom Meta editor on the Visual tab.
 3. Press the meta box's '''Move down''' arrow.
 4. The editor is now blank.
 5. Click '''Text''', then '''Visual'''.

== Expected ==

Reordering a meta box leaves the editors inside it working, with their content intact.

== Actual ==

The editor is blank from step 3 on, and step 5 throws:

{{{
Uncaught TypeError: Cannot read properties of null (reading 'setBaseAndExtent')
    at Object.select (tinymce.min.js:2:293163)
    at _ (editor.min.js?ver=7.1:2:5063)
    at n (editor.min.js?ver=7.1:2:1731)
}}}

Measured either side of step 3 with:

{{{#!js
const ed  = tinymce.get( 'my-custom-meta' );
const ifr = document.getElementById( 'my-custom-meta_ifr' );
( {
	parent:     document.getElementById( 'my-custom-metabox' ).parentElement.id,
	sameDoc:    ed.getDoc() === ifr.contentDocument,
	iframeBody: ifr.contentDocument.body.innerHTML,
	content:    ed.getContent(),
} )
}}}

|| '''''' || '''Before''' || '''After''' ||
|| postbox parent || `normal-sortables` || `advanced-sortables` ||
|| `getDoc() === contentDocument` || `true` || `false` ||
|| iframe body || `<p>…</p>` || `""` ||
|| `getContent()` || `<p>…</p>` || `<p>…</p>` (stranded) ||

== Environment ==

WordPress 7.1, Chrome 151, macOS. Reproduced both with the block editor and with Classic Editor 1.7.0 and `classic-editor-replace = classic`, i.e. with the block editor entirely out of the page.

== Related ==

Very likely the underlying cause of the `setBaseAndExtent` reports on #62450. That ticket is framed as a Firefox-only failure to initialise on the Visual tab, but the comments that reopened it describe this exact symptom — content disappearing on Visual/Text switching with `can't access property "setBaseAndExtent", n is null` — from users of ACF/SCF/JetEngine WYSIWYG fields, which render `wp_editor()` inside meta boxes and relocate their wrappers in the DOM.

I have not shown that this explains the original Firefox initialise-on-Visual report, and I have only tested Chrome, so I am filing separately rather than assuming they are one bug.

Originally surfaced from [https://github.com/WordPress/gutenberg/issues/74627 Gutenberg #74627].

Hello and best regards.
I am a WordPress plugin developer. I was working on a plugin when, following a PHP update from version 7.4 to 8.2 (or higher), I encountered a strange error:

{{{
Fatal error: Uncaught TypeError: array_merge(): Argument #1 must be of type array, null given in /.../wp-includes/rest-api/class-wp-rest-server.php:1166
Stack trace:
#0 /.../wp-includes/rest-api/class-wp-rest-server.php(1166): array_merge(NULL)
}}}

In fact, line 1166 of the `class-wp-rest-server.php` file attempts to perform an `array_merge` without considering the variable's value or applying any specific validation; I resolved the error quite simply with a minor change:

Original: `$routes = array_merge( ...$with_namespace );`
Edited (Fixed): 
{{{
                        //Fixed by MDesign
			foreach ( $with_namespace as $namespace_routes ) {
				if ( is_array( $namespace_routes ) ) {
					$routes = array_merge( $routes, $namespace_routes );
				}
			}
			/**/
}}}

WP_Upgrader::maintenance_mode() only initializes the filesystem if $wp_filesystem isn't already set:

{{{
if ( ! $wp_filesystem ) {
    WP_Filesystem();
}
}}}


This assumes that if the global is already an object, it must be properly connected. That assumption doesn't hold: if something earlier in the same request leaves a WP_Filesystem_FTPext instance in that global without connect() ever having been called on it (or after a connection that's since gone stale), maintenance_mode() uses it anyway. The next filesystem call that needs a live connection — in our case WP_Filesystem_Base::find_folder() → WP_Filesystem_FTPext::exists() — passes the unset $this->link straight into ftp_nlist(), which fatals under PHP 8's strict internal-function typing:

{{{
PHP Fatal error:  Uncaught TypeError: ftp_nlist(): Argument #1 ($ftp) must be of type FTP\Connection, null given in wp-admin/includes/class-wp-filesystem-ftpext.php:438
Stack trace:
#0 wp-admin/includes/class-wp-filesystem-ftpext.php(438): ftp_nlist()
#1 wp-admin/includes/class-wp-filesystem-base.php(231): WP_Filesystem_FTPext->exists()
#2 wp-admin/includes/class-wp-filesystem-base.php(58): WP_Filesystem_Base->find_folder()
#3 wp-admin/includes/class-wp-upgrader.php(1025): WP_Filesystem_Base->abspath()
#4 wp-admin/includes/class-wp-automatic-updater.php(474): WP_Upgrader->maintenance_mode()
#5 wp-admin/includes/class-wp-automatic-updater.php(676): WP_Automatic_Updater->update()
#6 wp-includes/update.php(890): WP_Automatic_Updater->run()
#7 (via wp_maybe_auto_update → wp_version_check, triggered under real WP-Cron)
  thrown in wp-admin/includes/class-wp-filesystem-ftpext.php on line 438
}}}

Because maintenance_mode() is called once per WP_Automatic_Updater::update() invocation — not per attempted file operation — this single uncaught fatal aborts the entire automatic-update run for every item scheduled in that cycle (core, every opted-in plugin, every opted-in theme), not just the one that happened to trigger it.

Confirmed NOT the cause (ruled out during investigation, worth noting so triage doesn't re-tread this): FTP credentials (verified valid via direct FTP login with the exact stored values), disable_functions, the ftp extension not being loaded, and DNS/IPv6 resolution ambiguity on the configured FTP host (php_network_connect_socket_to_host() in main/network.c already iterates and falls back across resolved addresses correctly). 

A parallel, manually-constructed WP_Filesystem_FTPext object using the site's real credentials connects successfully every time it's tested — including one second before an observed real crash in the same request — confirming the defect is specifically about whether connect() gets called on the object that ends up being used, not whether a connection can succeed.

What's not yet isolated: the exact condition that leaves the earlier $wp_filesystem unconnected in the first place (it's intermittent, not deterministic). request_filesystem_credentials() (called from WP_Upgrader::fs_connect(), which does always call WP_Filesystem()) seems like the most likely place to look, but this hasn't been confirmed.

=== Suggested fix ===

maintenance_mode()'s check should verify the existing $wp_filesystem is actually usable, not just non-null — e.g. checking connection state where the concrete class exposes one, or simply always calling WP_Filesystem() here rather than conditionally skipping it (since a fresh connect is cheap relative to the cost of silently aborting the whole update run).

Workaround in use (mu-plugin, not a core patch):


{{{
add_action('wp_maybe_auto_update', function () {
    global $wp_filesystem;
    if ($wp_filesystem instanceof WP_Filesystem_FTPext) {
        $needs_reconnect = empty($wp_filesystem->link) || @ftp_pwd($wp_filesystem->link) === false;
        if ($needs_reconnect) {
            $wp_filesystem = null; // forces maintenance_mode()'s check to do a fresh, real connect
        }
    }
}, 1);
}}}


This has been running on the previously failing site and confirmed to allow automatic updates to complete successfully on a previously-reliably-crashing site.

The description of the {{{wp_filter_object_list()}}} uses the uppercase form of the operator:

{{{#!php
 * If an object has more properties than those specified in arguments,
 * that will not disqualify it. When using the 'AND' operator,
 * any missing properties will disqualify it.
}}}

The {{{$operator}}} parameter description likewise documents the accepted values and default using uppercase:

{{{#!php
 * @param string      $operator Optional. The logical operation to perform. 'AND' means
 *                              all elements from the array must match. 'OR' means only
 *                              one element needs to match. 'NOT' means no elements may
 *                              match. Default 'AND'.
}}}

Both the DocBlock description and the {{{$operator}}} parameter description therefore indicate that uppercase operators should be used.

However, the function declaration uses the lowercase default {{{'and'}}}:

{{{#!php
function wp_filter_object_list( $input_list, $args = array(), $operator = 'and', $field = false )
}}}

The documentation therefore contradicts the function declaration regarding the casing of the default value and does not explain that the operator is case-insensitive.

{{{wp_filter_object_list()}}} passes {{{$operator}}} to {{{WP_List_Util::filter()}}}, which normalizes it to uppercase before validation:

{{{#!php
$operator = strtoupper( $operator );

if ( ! in_array( $operator, array( 'AND', 'OR', 'NOT' ), true ) ) {
	$this->output = array();
	return $this->output;
}
}}}

Consequently, lowercase, uppercase, and mixed-case variants produce the same result.

At a minimum, the DocBlock for {{{wp_filter_object_list()}}} should state the actual default and document that the argument is case-insensitive. For example:

{{{#!php
 * @param string      $operator Optional. The logical operation to perform. 'AND' means
 *                              all elements from the array must match. 'OR' means only
 *                              one element needs to match. 'NOT' means no elements may
 *                              match. The value is case-insensitive. Default 'and'.
}}}

== Broader inconsistency ==

As a related issue, casing is not used consistently across the public functions and methods that ultimately pass {{{$operator}}} to {{{WP_List_Util::filter()}}}:

|| '''Function or method''' || '''Default value''' || '''Documented values''' ||
|| {{{get_post_types()}}} || {{{'and'}}} || {{{'and'}}}, {{{'or'}}}, {{{'not'}}} ||
|| {{{get_post_stati()}}} || {{{'and'}}} || {{{'and'}}}, {{{'or'}}} ||
|| {{{get_taxonomies()}}} || {{{'and'}}} || {{{'and'}}}, {{{'or'}}} ||
|| {{{get_post_types_by_support()}}} || {{{'and'}}} || {{{'and'}}}, {{{'or'}}}, {{{'not'}}} ||
|| {{{wp_filter_object_list()}}} || {{{'and'}}} || {{{'AND'}}}, {{{'OR'}}}, {{{'NOT'}}} ||
|| {{{wp_list_filter()}}} || {{{'AND'}}} || {{{'AND'}}}, {{{'OR'}}}, {{{'NOT'}}} ||
|| {{{WP_List_Util::filter()}}} || {{{'AND'}}} || {{{'AND'}}}, {{{'OR'}}}, {{{'NOT'}}} ||

Although these differences do not affect the filtering results, they make the accepted values and their case sensitivity unclear without tracing the complete call chain.

A broader follow-up could establish consistent casing across these functions.

